'''Napier''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]]. It is named after the city of [[Napier, New Zealand|Napier]], the main urban area within the electorate. The electorate was established for the [[1860–1861 New Zealand general election|1861 election]] and has existed since. Neighbouring electorates are [[Tukituki (electorate)|Tukituki]] to the south, [[East Coast (electorate)|East Coast]] to the north, and [[Taupō (electorate)|Taupō]] and [[Rangitīkei (electorate)|Rangitīkei]] over the ranges to the west.

It has been held by [[Katie Nimon]] of the [[New Zealand National Party]] since the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]]. It was held by [[Stuart Nash]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party]] from the [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014 general election]] until 2023, when he did not stand for re-election.

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Napier.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The electorate stretches from the [[Ngaruroro River]] in the south, up through all of the [[Wairoa District]], to [[Muriwai, Gisborne|Muriwai]], almost to the outskirts of [[Gisborne, New Zealand|Gisborne]], and inland to [[Mātāwai]] at its northernmost point.

It includes the following population centres:
* [[Napier, New Zealand|Napier]]
* [[Pakowhai]]
* [[Waiohiki]]
* [[Omahu]]
* [[Sherenden]]
* [[Wairoa]]
* [[Frasertown]]
* [[Nūhaka]]
* [[Tuai]]

==History==
The electorate was created in 1861, and preceded by the {{NZ electorate link|Wairarapa and Hawke's Bay}} electorate from 1853 to 1860 and then briefly the {{NZ electorate link|County of Hawke}} electorate in 1860. It was a two-member electorate from 1876 to 1881.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=161}}

The first representative was [[Henry Powning Stark]], who won the election on 19 February 1861.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Elections |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=HBH18610223.2.16 |access-date=24 January 2014 |work=[[Hawke's Bay Herald]] |date=23 February 1861 |volume=4 |issue=179 |page=5}}</ref>

There were speculations that [[Douglas Maclean]] would be the conservative candidate in the Napier electorate in the {{NZ election link|1890}} upon his return from England instead of [[George Swan (politician)|George Swan]], but this was not correct.<ref>{{cite news |title= Election News |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=PBH18901022.2.17 |access-date=21 May 2013 |work=Poverty Bay Herald |volume=XVIII |issue=5902 |date=22 October 1890 |page=2}}</ref> Swan contested the election and was successful against the [[New Zealand Liberal Party|Liberal Party]] candidate Michael Gannon.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=237}}<ref>{{cite web |title=The General Election, 1890 |url= https://atojs.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/atojs?a=d&cl=search&d=AJHR1891-I.2.1.6.2 |publisher=National Library |access-date=25 February 2012 |page=1 |year=1891}}</ref> In the {{NZ election link|1893}}, Swan was challenged by the Liberal Party candidate [[Samuel Carnell]], with the latter being successful against the incumbent.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=188}}<ref>{{cite web |title=The General Election, 1893 |url= https://atojs.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/atojs?a=d&cl=search&d=AJHR1894-I.2.3.2.21 |publisher=National Library |access-date=25 February 2012 |page=1 |year=1894}}</ref> In the {{NZ election link|1896}}, Carnell in turn was challenged by the conservative candidate Douglas Maclean, with Maclean achieving a large majority against the incumbent.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=217}}<ref>{{cite news |title=The General Election |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=PBH18961205.2.11 |access-date=21 May 2013 |work=Poverty Bay Herald |volume=XXIII |issue=7794 |date=5 December 1896 |page=2}}</ref>

In the {{NZ election link|1931}}, the incumbent, [[Bill Barnard]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]], was challenged by John Butler of the [[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform Party]] as the official candidate of the [[United–Reform Coalition]], and [[United Party (New Zealand)|United Party]] member [[Vigor Brown]] as an independent. Brown, at the time [[Mayor of Napier, New Zealand|Mayor of Napier]] and previously MP for Napier for many years, withdrew just before the election, but too late for his name to be excluded from the ballot papers.<ref>{{cite news |title=Candidate Withdraws |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=AS19311125.2.118 |access-date=26 November 2014 |work=[[Auckland Star]] |volume=LXII |issue=279 |date=25 November 1931 |page=11}}</ref> The election was won by Barnard.<ref name="1931 election" />

Labour's [[Russell Fairbrother]] was first elected in the electorate in the [[2002 New Zealand general election|2002 election]], replacing long-standing MP [[Geoff Braybrooke]]. In the {{NZ election link|2005}}, [[Chris Tremain]] defeated Fairbrother, winning the electorate for the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] for the first time since the {{NZ election link|1951}}.<ref name="2005 Napier election" /> In the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]], Tremain retained the electorate with an increased majority over Fairbrother.<ref name="2008 Napier election" /> In the {{NZ election link|2011}}, Tremain beat Labour's [[Stuart Nash]].<ref name="2011 Napier election" />

Tremain announced in September 2013 that he would not contest the 2014 election.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA1309/S00530/chris-tremain-not-contesting-2014-election.htm |title=Chris Tremain Not Contesting 2014 Election |publisher=Scoop |date=30 September 2013 |access-date=30 September 2013}}</ref> Wayne Walford succeeded Tremain as National's candidate for the seat,<ref>{{cite news |last=Watkins |first=Tracy |title=Labour announces Chch Central candidate |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/city-centre/9808282/Labour-announces-Chch-Central-candidate |access-date=10 March 2014 |work=[[The Press]] |date=10 March 2014 |page=A2}}</ref> Nash contested the electorate for the Labour Party for the second time, and [[Garth McVicar]] stood for the [[Conservative Party of New Zealand|Conservative Party]]. McVicar had a high-profile due to his previous involvement with the [[Sensible Sentencing Trust]] lobby group. In July 2014, Walford was referred to Police by the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] for breaching the Electoral Act by failing to display an authorisation statement on his campaign vehicle.<ref>{{cite web |title=Referral to the Police 21 July 2014 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/referral-police-21-july-2014 |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=12 August 2014 |date=21 July 2014}}</ref>

Nash had a majority of 3,850 votes over Walford.<ref name="2014 final">{{cite web |title=Election Results – Napier |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-29.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=4 October 2014 |date=4 October 2014}}</ref> McVicar's 7,603 votes [[vote splitting|split]] the traditional National Party votes (24.8% of electors who gave their party vote to National gave their electorate vote to McVicar, a total of 4,465 votes),<ref>{{cite web|url=http://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/elect-splitvote-29.html|title=2014 General Election Split Voting Statistics – Napier|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]|access-date=5 December 2014}}</ref> which helped Nash win the election.<ref name="2014 final" /><ref>{{cite news| first=Simon |last=Hendery| url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/hawkes-bay-today/news/article.cfm?c_id=1503462&objectid=11328621 |title=Napier returns to Labour, Nash returns to Parliament |work=[[Hawke's Bay Today]] |date=20 September 2014 |access-date=22 September 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/hawkes-bay-today/opinion/news/article.cfm?c_id=1503459&objectid=11329372 |title=Bruce Wills: Crucial factors show the best team won |first=Bruce |last=Wills| work=[[Hawke's Bay Today]] |date=22 September 2014 |access-date=22 September 2014}}</ref>

In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would gain the communities north of the [[Ngaruroro River]] from {{NZ electorate link|Tukituki}} for the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]] onwards.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=3 September 2025 |page=8 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

